# punar-lock — the PAM stack the session lock authenticates against.
#
# Consumer: shell/punar-shell/Lock/Lock.qml, which probes for THIS FILE and
# uses `punar-lock` when it exists, falling back to `login` when it does
# not. The fallback works, but `login` is the console-login stack: it
# carries pam_nologin and a session phase that has no business running for
# a screen unlock. This file is the stack that says what a screen unlock
# actually is — "prove you are the user who is already logged in", nothing
# more.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE:
#   - no `session` lines. Unlocking does not open a session; the session is
#     already open. pam_systemd here would try to register a second one.
#   - no `password` lines. This stack never changes a passphrase.
#   - no pam_nologin. /etc/nologin is about arriving; the user is already
#     here, and locking a machine you cannot then unlock is a trap.
#
# pam_faillock is included as Arch ships it in system-auth: it rate-limits
# a passphrase-guessing attack against an unattended locked screen, which
# is precisely the threat this surface exists for. `preauth` refuses while
# the account is locked out, `authfail` counts a failure, `authsucc` clears
# the counter.
#
# No `nullok`: the image gives the dev user a real password
# (mkosi.postinst.chroot), and a lock that opens on an empty passphrase is
# not a lock. No `try_first_pass` either — nothing runs before pam_unix
# here, so there is no earlier token for it to try.
#
# pam_unix authenticates an UNPRIVILEGED process through the setuid
# `unix_chkpwd` helper, so punar-shell needs no privilege of its own to run
# this stack — the shell never reads a hash and never compares a string.

auth      required  pam_faillock.so    preauth
auth      [success=1 default=bad]  pam_unix.so
auth      [default=die]  pam_faillock.so  authfail
auth      required  pam_faillock.so    authsucc
account   required  pam_unix.so
